#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:94
msgid "heap_trim(): returning unused heap memory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:96
msgid ""
":func:`psutil.heap_trim` provides a cross-platform way to request that the "
"underlying allocator free any unused memory it's holding in the heap "
"(typically small ``malloc()`` allocations)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:100
msgid ""
"In practice, modern allocators rarely comply, so this is not a general-"
"purpose memory-reduction tool and won't meaningfully shrink :term:`RSS` in "
"real programs. Its primary value is in leak detection tools. Calling :func:"
"`psutil.heap_trim` before taking measurements helps reduce allocator noise, "
"giving you a cleaner baseline so that changes in :field:`heap_used` come "
"from the code you're testing, not from internal allocator caching or "
"fragmentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:109
msgid "Real-world use: finding a C extension leak"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:111
msgid "The workflow is simple:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:113
msgid "Take a baseline snapshot of the heap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:114
msgid "Call the C extension hundreds of times."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:115
msgid "Take another snapshot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:116
msgid "Compare."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:118
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"psutil.heap_trim()  # reduce noise\n"
"\n"
"before = psutil.heap_info()\n"
"for _ in range(200):\n"
"    my_cext_function()\n"
"after = psutil.heap_info()\n"
"\n"
"print(\"delta heap_used =\", after.heap_used - before.heap_used)\n"
"print(\"delta mmap_used =\", after.mmap_used - before.mmap_used)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:132
msgid ""
"If :field:`heap_used` or :field:`mmap_used` values increase consistently, "
"you've found a native leak."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:135
msgid ""
"To reduce false positives, repeat the test multiple times, increasing the "
"number of calls on each retry. This approach helps distinguish real leaks "
"from random noise or transient allocations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:140
msgid "A new tool: psleak"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:142
msgid ""
"The strategy described above is exactly what I implemented in a new PyPI "
"package, which I called `psleak <https://github.com/giampaolo/psleak>`__. It "
"runs the target function repeatedly, trims the allocator before each run, "
"and tracks differences across retries. Memory that grows consistently after "
"several runs is flagged as a leak."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:148
msgid "A minimal test suite looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2025/heap-introspection-apis.rst:150
msgid ""
"from psleak import MemoryLeakTestCase\n"
"\n"
"class TestLeaks(MemoryLeakTestCase):\n"
"    def test_fun(self):\n"
"        self.execute(some_c_function)"
msgstr ""

